module Core

import Native

{-
   The groups, written by the finite kernel's group loop (`Base64Kernel.yana`'s `encodeBase64Groups`)
   straight into the string's own units: natively a `String` is its UTF-8 bytes, so the reserved run
   is a byte window, and the kernel's block body - `Base64Kernel.native.yana`'s, at the natural width
   since J238 - is this one's too (D5, J202 §4). Until J202 this file and
   `Base64.native.avx512.yana` held their own copies of those two bodies over a `%CodeUnit`, the same
   loops with a different stop.

   The length is set before the kernel runs and the window is the run it reserved, so nothing is
   zero-filled - the kernel writes every unit of it, since the room is exactly the groups'. JavaScript
   keeps `Base64.yana`'s `pushUnit` loop: its units are UTF-16 and have no byte window (Analysis-
   Streams §6.8).
-}
@replaces
fn pushBase64Groups(bytes: [U8], url: Bool, &to: String) -> Size:
    let groups = length(bytes) / 3
    if groups <= 0 then return 0

    String.reserve(to, groups * 4)

    let start = to.units.length :: Size
    let wanted = start + groups * 4
    checkCondition((to.units.run.capacity :: Size) < wanted)
    to.units.length = truncate(wanted) :: Count

    let &window = ArrayView {items: bitcast(to.units.run.items + start) :: %U8, length: groups * 4}
    return encodeBase64Groups(url, bytes, window) * 3

{-
   The block form of the reader: the stream decoder's block bodies (`decodeBase64Blocks`, at the natural
   width in `Base64Kernel.native.yana` since J238, and sixty-four lanes in `.native.avx512.yana`) asked
   for **either alphabet**,
   and lent the array's reserved bytes as the window - the dual of `pushBase64Groups` above, and J234's
   rebasing of this reader onto the kernel as J202 rebased the writer. Until then this was its own
   sixteen-lane loop that classified each unit by range tests, both alphabets at once, and there was no
   wider one; the kernel's nibble tables take one alphabet, and `either` remaps `-` and `_` onto `+` and
   `/` in front of the standard one.

   What comes back is a group boundary with exactly the groups' bytes appended, which is all
   `Base64.yana`'s walk needs: `=`, a line break, a unit of neither alphabet and a run too short for a
   block are the walk's. The window is three bytes per four units of what is left and four more, since
   the sixteen-lane store writes a register for twelve bytes and wants that room for the last block. The
   natural width's store wants a whole register for three quarters of one, and where the window is
   short of that the sixteen-lane tail takes the block instead.
-}
@replaces
fn pushBase64Blocks(text: String, from: Size, &bytes: Array(U8)) -> Size:
    let units = length(text)
    if from + 16 > units then return from

    let held = bytes.length :: Size
    let room = ((units - from) / 4) * 3 + 4
    reserve(bytes, held + room)

    let &window = ArrayView {items: bytes.run.items + held, length: room}
    let run = decodeBase64Blocks(False, True, String.bytes(text)[from..units], window)

    bytes.length = truncate(held + run.written) :: Count
    return from + run.read
